item.description:
In Rust, macros are a powerful metaprogramming feature that let you write code that writes code. They allow you to define patterns and generate code at compile time, which can reduce boilerplate, improve readability, or enable complex functionality.Metaprogramming
Concept in computer programming where, a program can treat other programs as input data
There are 3 kinds of macros in rust:The most straight forward macros<img class="code-styler-icon is-unresolved" src="blob:/6266c130-7268-4c91-88e4-b0566fcd3060" target="_self">1macro_rules! say_hello { // Macros are created using macro_rules! macro2 () =&gt; {3 println!("Hello!");4 };5}67fn main() {8 say_hello!(); // Prints "Hello!"9}These are more powerful and flexible, and work on Rust code as input (AST manipulation). They're like compiler plugins which generate code from macros.<br><img class="code-styler-icon is-unresolved" src="blob:/6266c130-7268-4c91-88e4-b0566fcd3060" target="_self">1use proc_macro;23#[some_attribute]4pub fn some_name(input: TokenStream) -&gt; TokenStream {5}It prints the value of an expression along with the file name and line number where it’s used.<br><img class="code-styler-icon is-unresolved" src="blob:/6266c130-7268-4c91-88e4-b0566fcd3060" target="_self">1fn main() {2 let a = 2;3 let b = dbg!(a * 3) + 1;45 dbg!(b);6}78/* Output9[src/main.rs:4] a * 3 = 610[src/main.rs:6] b = 711*/Can be passed written in the arms of a conditional where you are sure that the control won't reach to silence the return requirements by the compiler<br><img class="code-styler-icon is-unresolved" src="blob:/6266c130-7268-4c91-88e4-b0566fcd3060" target="_self">Example1fn describe_day(day: u8) -&gt; &amp;'static str {2 match day {3 1..=5 =&gt; "Weekday",4 6 | 7 =&gt; "Weekend",5 _ =&gt; unreachable!("'day' should be between 1 and 7"),6 }7}89fn main() {10 println!("{}", describe_day(2)); // Weekday11 println!("{}", describe_day(6)); // Weekend12 // println!("{}", describe_day(8)); // Panics with message13}Crash the program<br><img class="code-styler-icon is-unresolved" src="blob:/6266c130-7268-4c91-88e4-b0566fcd3060" target="_self">1panic!(":(");

A range is a sequence of consecutive values.similar to python range<img class="code-styler-icon is-unresolved" src="blob:/6266c130-7268-4c91-88e4-b0566fcd3060" target="_self">1let my_range = 1..5;
Below table summarizes range types in rust- Ranges implement Iterator, so anything which implements that can have be the type in Range&lt;T&gt;
<br><img class="code-styler-icon is-unresolved" src="blob:/6266c130-7268-4c91-88e4-b0566fcd3060" target="_self">1let my_char_range = 'b'..'q';
Ranges can be used for Array Slicing
<br><img class="code-styler-icon is-unresolved" src="blob:/6266c130-7268-4c91-88e4-b0566fcd3060" target="_self">Array Slicing Example1let arr = [0, 1, 2, 3];2assert_eq!(arr[1..3], [1,2]);Below is an example with a for loop<br><img class="code-styler-icon is-unresolved" src="blob:/6266c130-7268-4c91-88e4-b0566fcd3060" target="_self">Range Iteration Example1for i in 1..=6 {2	println!(i);3}4for i in (1..=6).rev() {5 // Methods are present on Range type6 // Others include .step_by(val)7}
